00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:56.168
as a potential problem
on tomorrow's quiz, yes.

00:03:56.168 --> 00:03:58.137
You might want to know
about some of the things

00:03:58.137 --> 00:03:58.905
we talked about Monday.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:03:59.839
Here's an example.

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.975
OK, here's a question.

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.209
I have two glasses.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.044
They form from liquids,
and they're cooled

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.879
to some temperature I call T1.

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.514
And I tell you
that T1 is greater

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:11.317
than Tm, which is the melting
point of the material.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:15.855
Now remember, the melting
point is the same.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:17.123
It's a number.

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:21.327
The melting point of
silica, that's a number.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:26.466
But when the material
forms a glass can vary.

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.701
Why does a glass form?

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.703
Well, a glass forms--

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.272
this is what we
covered on Monday-- it

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:35.775
can form because of three
different things, right?

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.177
One is the crystal complexity.

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:43.616
That's important.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:49.255
Another is the viscosity.

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:51.691
And the third is
the cooling rate.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.731
Those are the three
things that we covered

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:04.103
on Monday for reasons
why a glass might form,

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:07.473
and then we started kind of
talking about the cooling rate.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.275
That's what we
need to understand

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:11.077
to answer this question.

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:14.213
So, OK, two glasses
forming liquids.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.048
One glass is formed.

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:18.017
Plot the molar volume
versus temperature.

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:20.320
All right, so now, let's see.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.354
How about this?

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:26.326
Could this be the volume
per mole versus temperature?

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:27.827
So I'm going to try it out here.

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:29.095
I'm going to go like this.

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:34.167
I'm going to go like this, and
that looks like the liquid,

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:35.868
maybe.

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:40.740
And then I'm going to go
like this and maybe this,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.477
and we'll call that T1.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:47.714
Does that look right?

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:51.217
No, this can't be right.

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:55.755
This can't be right because,
you see, I said that T1,

00:05:55.755 --> 00:06:00.760
this is a glass transition.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.028
That's a glass transition.

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:04.230
That's a Tg.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.065
That's another Tg.

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:11.704
But I've said in the question
T1 is greater than Tm.

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.339
T1 is greater than Tm.

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:18.711
Well, so this can't be true
because Tm has to be out here.

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.948
Tm is out here where there's
a different transition that

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:24.650
occurs, and it turns
into a crystal.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.955
So this couldn't be
right, T1 greater than Tm.

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.092
So what about another one?

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:34.659
Let's try another one.

00:06:34.659 --> 00:06:36.195
So how about if I--

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:38.865
OK, volume per mole.

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:40.666
Now I'm going to go like this.

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:42.568
So I'm going to go like this.

00:06:46.372 --> 00:06:51.878
So now here comes one,
and here comes the other.

00:06:55.248 --> 00:07:01.154
And OK, so I'm going
to put now T1 maybe--

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:08.461
let's try T1 is going to be
out here, and Tm would be--

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:10.129
actually, let me
do it differently.

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:10.997
Sorry.

00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:14.100
I'm going to go like this.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.705
I'll just connect-- how
do I want to do this?

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:21.841
I want it to join
these up there.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:24.377
Yeah, that's another
good mistake.

00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:26.379
I'm making mistakes on purpose.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.818
And let's make T1 be out here.

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:36.289
So T1 is here, and then
Tm could still be there.

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:38.457
This could be Tm.

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:41.494
And then maybe is that right?

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:43.362
Is that a possible curve?

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:46.232
Well, that fixed
the T1-Tm problem

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:50.837
because T1 is now greater than
Tm like I said in the question,

00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:53.773
but what is going on here?

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:55.107
What is going on?

00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:59.078
You say, well, but I cooled
one faster than the other.

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:00.947
Isn't that kind of
what's happening here?

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:04.183
Maybe I started
changing the curve?

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:05.051
No.

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:08.821
That's not what this is,
so don't make that mistake.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:17.230
You see, remember, the slope is
equal to the thermal expansion

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.533
coefficient--

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:23.836
thermal expansion coefficient.

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:27.206
And those slopes are the same
for the different glasses.

00:08:27.206 --> 00:08:30.142
Just because I've
cooled one differently,

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:31.944
they're not going to
have different slopes.

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:34.847
And this is the
fundamental challenge.

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:36.582
This is the fundamental
confusion point

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:40.620
that happens which is
that this is not time.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.123
This axis is not time.

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:50.396
So this is wrong to.

00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:53.699
What we're doing is we're
talking about something

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:56.469
involving time, cooling rate.

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:58.704
But we're plotting
it on something

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:01.374
that doesn't involve time, and
that can lead to confusion.

00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:02.742
So I wanted to point that out.

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:04.176
You say, well,
shouldn't the slope

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:06.946
be different because you're
cooling it slower or faster?

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:08.881
No, that's not
what this slope is.

00:09:08.881 --> 00:09:11.250
This slope is a thermal
expansion coefficient.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:15.421
So the right answer
to this must be

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:22.528
that you have your
nice liquid phase

00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:25.665
and you've made
your two glasses.

00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:28.301
So here's one.

00:09:28.301 --> 00:09:31.671
This has cooled faster.

00:09:31.671 --> 00:09:32.305
This is slower.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:38.177
This would be Tg.

00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:43.349
This would be another
Tg, maybe Tg1, Tg2.

00:09:43.349 --> 00:09:50.022
And the Tm would
come down and have--

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:53.459
well, I've run out
of room here, but Tm

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:55.394
might come down like this.

00:09:55.394 --> 00:09:58.364
And so T1 must be
somewhere up here.

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:03.636
That would be a correct
plot for that question.

00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:04.570
So does everybody see?

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:07.740
I just want you to get
a feeling for this plot.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.642
We introduced it
Monday, and I really

00:10:09.642 --> 00:10:13.412
want you to feel your
oneness with this plot

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:14.847
because it's a
very important plot

00:10:14.847 --> 00:10:18.451
to understand why glasses form.

00:10:18.451 --> 00:10:23.356
All right, now
moving on, there's

00:10:23.356 --> 00:10:25.825
a lot of properties of
glasses, and here I'm

00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:27.693
talking about oxide
glasses, so silica.

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:32.732
Remember, silicon oxide,
oxide, silica, alumina.

00:10:32.732 --> 00:10:35.868
These are, oh, oxide.

00:10:35.868 --> 00:10:37.169
We talked about that.

00:10:37.169 --> 00:10:39.071
Chemically inert,
electrically insulating,

00:10:39.071 --> 00:10:41.307
mechanically brittle,
optically transparent,

00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:43.676
visually arresting--

00:10:43.676 --> 00:10:45.678
I like that one--

00:10:45.678 --> 00:10:48.914
these are the
properties that we know

00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:50.750
about these kinds of glasses.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:51.617
We already know them.

00:10:51.617 --> 00:10:53.085
But what I want to
talk about today

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:56.455
is how they can be engineered.

00:10:56.455 --> 00:11:02.128
And this plot is a basis
for understanding that.

00:11:02.128 --> 00:11:06.699
And so is spaghetti, and that's
what we're going to talk about.

00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:09.068
So the very first one--
which we'll set up

00:11:09.068 --> 00:11:11.237
our demo in just a few minutes.

00:11:11.237 --> 00:11:13.806
The very first one that
I want to talk about

00:11:13.806 --> 00:11:15.141
is the mechanical properties.

00:11:15.141 --> 00:11:17.777
We know glass to
be very brittle,

00:11:17.777 --> 00:11:20.079
but could we change its
mechanical properties?

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:23.516
Could we do something about
the mechanical properties?

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:26.352
And by the way, if those
are the things that

00:11:26.352 --> 00:11:28.154
dictate whether a
glass forms or not,

00:11:28.154 --> 00:11:29.922
then those are the
things that you

00:11:29.922 --> 00:11:33.025
might think we might try to
engineer if we want to change

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:34.360
the properties of glass.

00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:42.401
You can imagine I'm a silica--
oh, you got to pass this out.

00:11:42.401 --> 00:11:46.906
I'm a big silicate,
an SiO4, and I'm

00:11:46.906 --> 00:11:49.040
connected to these
other silicate groups

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:56.649
through this shared bridged
oxygen. And I'm a bulky thing,

00:11:56.649 --> 00:12:01.420
and I'm trying to move around in
the liquid, crystal complexity.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:04.190
I'm cooling down, and I'm
trying to move around.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.492
And [INAUDIBLE] told
me to go that way,

00:12:06.492 --> 00:12:08.928
but it's actually over there,
or there's no building there

00:12:08.928 --> 00:12:10.129
and I don't know where to go.

00:12:10.129 --> 00:12:11.797
Where is the lattice site?

00:12:11.797 --> 00:12:13.232
It's complicated.

00:12:13.232 --> 00:12:14.900
Glass could form.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:16.102
Glass could form.

00:12:16.102 --> 00:12:18.104
Or maybe I'm a silicate
group and I'm like, hey,

00:12:18.104 --> 00:12:19.538
could you please
get out of my way?

00:12:19.538 --> 00:12:21.207
You're really
slowing things down,

00:12:21.207 --> 00:12:24.443
you other silicate group
that I'm attached to.

00:12:24.443 --> 00:12:26.412
Viscous, get out of my way.

00:12:26.412 --> 00:12:29.014
No, I can't, viscous.

00:12:29.014 --> 00:12:31.450
Or maybe you're a silicate
group and you're cooling down

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:35.755
and you're like,
that was very sudden.

00:12:35.755 --> 00:12:37.823
I didn't even realize
that happened.

00:12:37.823 --> 00:12:41.627
All of a sudden I'm
supposed to be a solid?

00:12:41.627 --> 00:12:42.928
This is what's happening.

00:12:42.928 --> 00:12:47.665
And so instead of getting into
this beautiful quartz crystal

00:12:47.665 --> 00:12:49.568
it doesn't get there.

00:12:49.568 --> 00:12:51.003
It doesn't get there.

00:12:51.003 --> 00:12:55.875
A supercooled liquid
wants to be a solid.

00:12:55.875 --> 00:13:01.847
It wants to, but it can't for
some reason, for some reason

00:13:01.847 --> 00:13:06.252
that has to do with those.

00:13:06.252 --> 00:13:08.187
So let's just have
this in our hands

00:13:08.187 --> 00:13:10.956
while we're listening and
thinking about things,

00:13:10.956 --> 00:13:15.628
and let me just tell
you about this one way--

00:13:15.628 --> 00:13:17.563
if I take a piece of glass--

00:13:17.563 --> 00:13:21.767
if I take a piece of
glass, and now imagine

00:13:21.767 --> 00:13:24.703
that I take some part of
it, like this part here.

00:13:27.473 --> 00:13:33.846
And in this part I'm
going to cool faster,

00:13:33.846 --> 00:13:40.186
and in this part here
I'm going to cool slower.

00:13:40.186 --> 00:13:47.226
So imagine it's a slab of glass,
and it's like the edges are

00:13:47.226 --> 00:13:49.528
cooled at a different rate.

00:13:49.528 --> 00:13:51.664
That's actually exactly
why a car window,

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:54.099
if you look at it through
polarized glasses,

00:13:54.099 --> 00:13:56.168
has spots on it.

00:13:56.168 --> 00:13:59.138
Those spots, some of
you may have seen these.

00:13:59.138 --> 00:14:03.475
Those spots are changes,
purposeful changes

00:14:03.475 --> 00:14:06.979
in the mechanical properties
of the glass to keep you safe.

00:14:06.979 --> 00:14:09.815
Why?

00:14:09.815 --> 00:14:14.987
If you call it slower, then
you might have some volume

00:14:14.987 --> 00:14:16.822
that you want.

00:14:16.822 --> 00:14:23.295
The volume here, let's call
this Vb and let's call this Va.

00:14:23.295 --> 00:14:27.967
That goes along with the
graph we used before, b and a.

00:14:27.967 --> 00:14:32.838
So it wants V--

00:14:32.838 --> 00:14:34.373
what did I do-- b.

00:14:34.373 --> 00:14:36.040
This wants Va.

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:37.076
But look at what happens.

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:42.982
If I purposely cool the
top really fast or maybe

00:14:42.982 --> 00:14:47.887
I make spots that I cool
fast, then what happens?

00:14:47.887 --> 00:14:52.224
Well, this wants to
have volume a inside.

00:14:52.224 --> 00:14:56.729
This wants to have volume--
well, this cools first.

00:14:56.729 --> 00:14:58.230
So this cools first.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:01.433
So inside, so what happens?

00:15:01.433 --> 00:15:07.406
As I cool it now, I have this.

00:15:07.406 --> 00:15:11.043
This is now a glass.

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:13.178
This is now a solid.

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:18.417
And I should say glass
solid, so it's solidified.

00:15:18.417 --> 00:15:24.223
And this wants to
have a lower volume--

00:15:24.223 --> 00:15:27.226
lower volume.

00:15:27.226 --> 00:15:29.428
It wants a lower
volume, but that's

00:15:29.428 --> 00:15:33.599
been frozen at a higher volume.

00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:34.199
Why?

00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:35.634
I cooled it faster.

00:15:35.634 --> 00:15:36.602
That's it.

00:15:36.602 --> 00:15:40.072
I cooled it faster, and now the
inside is like, wait a second,

00:15:40.072 --> 00:15:41.807
I'm not cooling so fast.

00:15:41.807 --> 00:15:43.642
I don't need such a high volume.

00:15:43.642 --> 00:15:45.911
Can I please get a smaller
volume out of this?

00:15:45.911 --> 00:15:49.081
And the top is like,
well, I'm already stuck.

00:15:49.081 --> 00:15:50.449
I'm already a solid.

00:15:50.449 --> 00:15:52.351
And what happens is it pulls.

00:15:56.322 --> 00:16:06.131
And so what you get is
wants lower volume pulls

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:11.870
on the surface, and that
means that this is going

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:16.175
to be the top, if I'll
just draw it one more time,

00:16:16.175 --> 00:16:18.310
will be under compression--

00:16:21.046 --> 00:16:25.017
under compression,
and the inside

00:16:25.017 --> 00:16:26.852
will be under tensile stress--

00:16:30.289 --> 00:16:31.523
under tensile stress.

00:16:31.523 --> 00:16:33.025
You can kind of
imagine this, right?

00:16:33.025 --> 00:16:34.226
You can see it.

00:16:34.226 --> 00:16:36.729
I've frozen the top
because of cooling rate.

00:16:36.729 --> 00:16:38.263
It's right here.

00:16:38.263 --> 00:16:39.999
I just cooled the top faster.

00:16:39.999 --> 00:16:41.633
The inside isn't cold yet.

00:16:41.633 --> 00:16:44.536
It starts to cool, and
it tries to solidify

00:16:44.536 --> 00:16:45.571
at a different volume.

00:16:45.571 --> 00:16:46.905
It pulls it in.

00:16:46.905 --> 00:16:51.043
But see, that changes
the mechanical properties

00:16:51.043 --> 00:16:52.578
of the surface.

00:16:52.578 --> 00:16:54.179
That's called tempering.

00:16:54.179 --> 00:16:56.148
That's called tempered glass.

00:16:56.148 --> 00:17:00.152
This is tempered glass, and
there is no better example

00:17:00.152 --> 00:17:05.723
of temporal glass than the
Prince Rupert's drop, which is

00:17:05.723 --> 00:17:08.493
the demo that Peter has come.

00:17:08.493 --> 00:17:09.194
Here's Peter.

00:17:09.194 --> 00:17:11.696
There's Peter
blowing some glass.

00:17:11.696 --> 00:17:14.665
This is what he does
in his free time.

00:17:14.665 --> 00:17:17.236
He just throws these big
glass-bowling parties.

00:17:17.236 --> 00:17:19.471
He also happens to be the
director of the glass lab,

00:17:19.471 --> 00:17:21.205
like I said.

00:17:21.205 --> 00:17:24.877
He also teaches the classes,
the glass-blowing classes.

00:17:24.877 --> 00:17:27.546
There's one for
freshmen during IAP,

00:17:27.546 --> 00:17:29.948
and there's another
one in the spring.

00:17:29.948 --> 00:17:30.983
They're very popular.

00:17:30.983 --> 00:17:35.654
But because you're in
3091, he'll get you in.

00:17:35.654 --> 00:17:39.491
He doesn't like it when I say
that because it's not true.

00:17:39.491 --> 00:17:41.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:44.463
But come talk to him.

00:17:44.463 --> 00:17:48.067
He's right here, and I think
you've got a demo for us.

00:17:48.067 --> 00:17:50.436
So I'm going to let
Peter take the stage

00:17:50.436 --> 00:17:55.174
and show you the best example
of tempered glass you can do.

00:17:55.174 --> 00:17:58.444
And if you want to use the mic--

00:17:58.444 --> 00:17:59.278
if you want to talk.

00:17:59.278 --> 00:18:06.585
--a little kit out

00:18:06.585 --> 00:18:08.654
And one year
Professor Cima, when

00:18:08.654 --> 00:18:12.691
he was teaching this
class, told the class

00:18:12.691 --> 00:18:15.094
that they had to come to the
lottery for the glassblowing

00:18:15.094 --> 00:18:18.864
class, and it's a big class.

00:18:18.864 --> 00:18:21.767
And so dutifully everyone
came to the lottery,

00:18:21.767 --> 00:18:25.971
and we already have about
150 students plus 3091.

00:18:25.971 --> 00:18:28.674
And the lottery
was in room 6120,

00:18:28.674 --> 00:18:32.511
so we overflowed it by a lot.

00:18:32.511 --> 00:18:36.014
So I told Michael, don't
ever do that again, please.

00:18:36.014 --> 00:18:38.817
Which is why I do it every year.

00:18:38.817 --> 00:18:40.452
But we like 3091 students.

00:18:40.452 --> 00:18:42.688
We'll try to give
them preference.

00:18:42.688 --> 00:18:44.056
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:44.056 --> 00:18:46.024
Trying to work it for you.

00:18:46.024 --> 00:18:46.992
He says that.

00:18:59.204 --> 00:19:02.908
How many of you have seen
Prince Rupert drops before?

00:19:02.908 --> 00:19:03.709
Some people, yeah.

00:19:03.709 --> 00:19:08.247
There's a lot of online
stuff about them,

00:19:08.247 --> 00:19:09.815
but you get to see
a live one today.

00:19:14.219 --> 00:19:17.122
So what's the difference
between a Prince Rupert drop

00:19:17.122 --> 00:19:21.660
and tempered glass like
the type [INAUDIBLE]??

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:25.230
Anybody want to take
a guess at that?

00:19:28.333 --> 00:19:30.035
They're both under
stress, but the stress

00:19:30.035 --> 00:19:32.337
is generated in a different way.

00:19:32.337 --> 00:19:34.907
So the dots that were
on that windshield

00:19:34.907 --> 00:19:36.375
are formed by air coolant.

00:19:36.375 --> 00:19:37.943
That's how tempered
glass is made.

00:19:37.943 --> 00:19:40.879
It goes through what's called
a [? lear ?] or a rolling

00:19:40.879 --> 00:19:43.649
conveyor, and it's heated
up very, very quickly,

00:19:43.649 --> 00:19:45.417
and then it's
cooled very quickly.

00:19:45.417 --> 00:19:49.054
And it's cooled by this
array of compressed air jets

00:19:49.054 --> 00:19:52.491
on both sides that take it
from almost the transition

00:19:52.491 --> 00:19:53.792
temperature--

00:19:53.792 --> 00:19:55.994
it just about wants
to move but doesn't

00:19:55.994 --> 00:20:00.999
quite, down to room temperature
in three minutes or so.

00:20:00.999 --> 00:20:05.604
And that super-rapid cooling
is what generates that stress

00:20:05.604 --> 00:20:06.371
on the--

00:20:06.371 --> 00:20:08.006
the difference
between the inside

00:20:08.006 --> 00:20:10.409
and the outside
temperature in the glass.

00:20:10.409 --> 00:20:12.444
And so it's air cooled,
but Prince Rupert

00:20:12.444 --> 00:20:16.582
drop is cooled by--
anyone want to guess?

00:20:16.582 --> 00:20:17.416
Water.

00:20:17.416 --> 00:20:19.051
Yeah, by water.

00:20:19.051 --> 00:20:22.020
So water is a better
coolant, right?

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:25.691
So as a result, you
get a lot more stress

00:20:25.691 --> 00:20:28.560
embodied with the glass,
a lot more stress.

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.996
And so that's
relevant because you

00:20:30.996 --> 00:20:34.233
don't want to make a window that
breaks with that kind of energy

00:20:34.233 --> 00:20:37.636
and so you get explosive
energy all over the place.

00:20:37.636 --> 00:20:40.372
So tempered glass is
engineered to fail.

00:20:40.372 --> 00:20:43.442
It's very strong,
but it's engineered

00:20:43.442 --> 00:20:46.278
to fail so that when it breaks,
it breaks into small pieces

00:20:46.278 --> 00:20:50.382
so that if you're walking along
the sidewalk and a skyscraper

00:20:50.382 --> 00:20:53.752
blows out a window, you don't
get huge shards of glass

00:20:53.752 --> 00:20:56.121
falling down and hurting people.

00:20:56.121 --> 00:20:58.423
You get little pellets
gently raining down,

00:20:58.423 --> 00:21:00.359
which isn't such a bad thing.

00:21:00.359 --> 00:21:01.793
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:01.793 --> 00:21:04.763
So I've got two kinds of Prince
Rupert drops that I made here

00:21:04.763 --> 00:21:08.800
and a vessel in which
to demonstrate here.

00:21:13.372 --> 00:21:15.374
So first I'm going to show you--

00:21:15.374 --> 00:21:19.711
they both cool rather quickly,
but one was cooled in air.

00:21:19.711 --> 00:21:22.881
One was cooled in water.

00:21:22.881 --> 00:21:27.119
This is the one
that's cooled in air.

00:21:27.119 --> 00:21:29.688
And by the way, I used to
give a demo in the Glass Lab

00:21:29.688 --> 00:21:32.824
where I would take the
Prince Rupert drop,

00:21:32.824 --> 00:21:35.227
put it on a sealed table
that we call the marver,

00:21:35.227 --> 00:21:37.329
and hit it with a hammer
as hard as I could.

00:21:37.329 --> 00:21:40.432
I was never able to shatter
this head with a hammer.

00:21:40.432 --> 00:21:42.734
In fact, I dented the
marver so many times

00:21:42.734 --> 00:21:45.170
that I stopped doing that demo.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:48.907
So I have a different way
of doing it now which is--

00:21:48.907 --> 00:21:50.509
I won't do that today,
but usually I'll

00:21:50.509 --> 00:21:52.778
have a student come and try
to break the Prince Rupert

00:21:52.778 --> 00:21:53.879
drop in their hands.

00:21:53.879 --> 00:21:55.080
It's really hard to do.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:56.581
Most people can't do it.

00:21:56.581 --> 00:21:59.351
But the air-cooled
Prince Rupert drop

00:21:59.351 --> 00:22:01.019
doesn't have the same
kind of strength.

00:22:01.019 --> 00:22:02.487
So watch what happens.

00:22:06.958 --> 00:22:10.429
Not much.

00:22:10.429 --> 00:22:11.797
Well, let's try down farther.

00:22:11.797 --> 00:22:13.131
Maybe there's more stress there.

00:22:15.801 --> 00:22:17.502
Nope, not [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:20.605 --> 00:22:23.241
Let me try one more time.

00:22:23.241 --> 00:22:25.610
Maybe where it's thicker maybe
there will be more stress.

00:22:29.548 --> 00:22:33.018
Maybe a little.

00:22:33.018 --> 00:22:34.853
So, here's the one
that's water cooled.

00:22:44.896 --> 00:22:47.332
OK, don't blink.

00:22:47.332 --> 00:22:52.170
Five, four, three, two, one.

00:22:58.143 --> 00:23:01.480
That's the speed
of sound in glass,

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:04.015
which is, I think, about three
times faster than the speed

00:23:04.015 --> 00:23:05.884
of sound in air.

00:23:05.884 --> 00:23:07.486
So you can't see it.

00:23:07.486 --> 00:23:09.921
But there is some great
high-speed video--

00:23:09.921 --> 00:23:12.157
maybe some of you have
seen that online--

00:23:12.157 --> 00:23:14.693
of Prince Rupert
drops exploding.

00:23:14.693 --> 00:23:16.661
And actually up in
the Edgerton Center

00:23:16.661 --> 00:23:19.364
they have a camera
that can catch that,

00:23:19.364 --> 00:23:21.867
and they've got some
really cool video.

00:23:21.867 --> 00:23:22.868
I'm going to leave this.

00:23:22.868 --> 00:23:24.469
This is a very
cool fragment that

00:23:24.469 --> 00:23:27.739
shows the fracture, the
failure pattern, which

00:23:27.739 --> 00:23:28.673
is really interesting.

00:23:28.673 --> 00:23:31.276
It fails from the
center of the drop

00:23:31.276 --> 00:23:33.812
radially out at an
angle that looks

00:23:33.812 --> 00:23:36.314
to be about a 45-degree angle.

00:23:36.314 --> 00:23:37.582
You can probably explain that.

00:23:37.582 --> 00:23:38.950
That will be on
the quiz tomorrow.

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:41.686
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:42.187 --> 00:23:44.055
45 degree [INAUDIBLE]?

00:23:44.055 --> 00:23:45.824
Was there a question?

00:23:45.824 --> 00:23:48.760
I'm going to do one more
just in case anybody blinked,

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:52.330
and then I'm going
to be on my way.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:53.965
By the way, Jeff
mentioned that we

00:23:53.965 --> 00:23:58.503
had an IAP pass that starts
during IAP for freshmen

00:23:58.503 --> 00:24:02.007
and a spring class that's open
to everybody at the institute.

00:24:02.007 --> 00:24:05.010
So if you're not a freshman,
you can come to the lottery.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:07.412
The lottery is for
all of our classes,

00:24:07.412 --> 00:24:09.581
are shown on the
Glass Lab website.

00:24:09.581 --> 00:24:11.049
And I think we're
going to be doing

00:24:11.049 --> 00:24:14.386
the lottery for the spring
for the IAP class in November

00:24:14.386 --> 00:24:19.157
late this month, and then the
spring class will probably

00:24:19.157 --> 00:24:22.527
be sometime in January,
and it starts in March.

00:24:22.527 --> 00:24:23.528
OK, ready?

00:24:23.528 --> 00:24:28.567
Five, four, three, two, one.

00:24:28.567 --> 00:24:32.037
[INAUDIBLE] will even
have some more energy.

00:24:35.941 --> 00:24:36.675
Thanks, Peter.

00:24:36.675 --> 00:24:40.679
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:47.519 --> 00:24:49.488
That was awesome,
and I actually do

00:24:49.488 --> 00:24:52.991
have a high-speed video of it,
so I'll show that to you now.

00:24:52.991 --> 00:24:56.962
And again, so if you have
questions about glass,

00:24:56.962 --> 00:24:57.863
if you want to watch--

00:24:57.863 --> 00:25:02.601
Peter can make animals for
you, I think, on request.

00:25:02.601 --> 00:25:05.871
Oh, this guy.

00:25:05.871 --> 00:25:08.006
But anyway, thanks a lot,
Peter, for coming by.

00:25:10.775 --> 00:25:16.081
As you know, I'm a big believer
in hands on, seeing things.

00:25:16.081 --> 00:25:17.849
Can we pass-- is this--

00:25:17.849 --> 00:25:18.617
no.

00:25:18.617 --> 00:25:20.051
Well, your call.

00:25:20.051 --> 00:25:23.655
Just don't eat it.

00:25:23.655 --> 00:25:28.293
So now you know on parent's
day, when we have parents here,

00:25:28.293 --> 00:25:29.594
we had the two sheets of glass.

00:25:29.594 --> 00:25:30.795
One is called annealed.

00:25:30.795 --> 00:25:34.199
It just means it's not
tempered, and the baseball

00:25:34.199 --> 00:25:36.668
was supposed to break it.

00:25:36.668 --> 00:25:38.837
It didn't.

00:25:38.837 --> 00:25:40.539
Peter's got very
good annealed glass.

00:25:40.539 --> 00:25:43.008
He's the one that gave
us those big sheets.

00:25:43.008 --> 00:25:46.444
And it breaks into these
huge, dangerous shards.

00:25:46.444 --> 00:25:47.045
Thanks, Peter.

00:25:47.045 --> 00:25:49.080
Take care.

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:50.515
Oh yeah, thank you.

00:25:50.515 --> 00:25:53.885
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:57.289 --> 00:26:01.593
If you notice, the tempered
glass, we tried to break it.

00:26:01.593 --> 00:26:03.495
It also doesn't break.

00:26:03.495 --> 00:26:06.097
It was supposed to be the
only one that didn't break.

00:26:06.097 --> 00:26:08.266
But then you take a
little hammer at the edge

00:26:08.266 --> 00:26:11.102
and you just lightly tap
the edge and the whole thing

00:26:11.102 --> 00:26:11.603
breaks.

00:26:11.603 --> 00:26:12.203
Why?

00:26:12.203 --> 00:26:15.907
Because it's got so
much built-in stress.

00:26:15.907 --> 00:26:18.310
It's got all this
compressive stress,

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:21.680
and the edge isn't
protecting that.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:24.749
The edge of that tempered glass
isn't protecting the stress.

00:26:24.749 --> 00:26:26.785
So when I just dig
in a little bit,

00:26:26.785 --> 00:26:30.722
it's able to all crack
and release that stress.

00:26:30.722 --> 00:26:32.591
So that's what
happened on family day.

00:26:32.591 --> 00:26:33.825
Here's a high-speed video.

00:26:33.825 --> 00:26:35.660
These are two guys from Corning.

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:37.762
And we can listen to this.

00:26:37.762 --> 00:26:38.430
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:39.731
They're actually pretty cool.

00:26:39.731 --> 00:26:42.801
- So there's a few
things going on here.

00:26:42.801 --> 00:26:46.671
The cold water rapidly cools the
exterior surface of the glass,

00:26:46.671 --> 00:26:48.873
hardening it almost immediately.

00:26:48.873 --> 00:26:52.344
The interior, still
molten, cools more slowly.

00:26:52.344 --> 00:26:55.513
As it cools, it contracts and
attempts to pull the surface

00:26:55.513 --> 00:26:57.449
in with it, but it can't--

00:26:57.449 --> 00:26:58.950
well, not very much.

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:00.619
The surface has
already hardened,

00:27:00.619 --> 00:27:03.622
so it gets pulled in only
a little, compressing it,

00:27:03.622 --> 00:27:07.158
while also creating an internal
layer that remains forever

00:27:07.158 --> 00:27:09.227
under tension.

00:27:09.227 --> 00:27:10.929
It is this action
that gives the glass

00:27:10.929 --> 00:27:12.897
its uncharacteristic strength.

00:27:12.897 --> 00:27:16.334
We call it compressive strength.

00:27:16.334 --> 00:27:17.802
- It sounds like
the same principle

00:27:17.802 --> 00:27:20.972
as how an arch provides strength
in structural engineering.

00:27:20.972 --> 00:27:22.040
- Yes, kind of.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:23.842
Now, Jamie, I'm going
to ask for your help.

00:27:23.842 --> 00:27:26.277
We're going to attempt to
destroy this Prince Rupert

00:27:26.277 --> 00:27:26.978
drop.

00:27:26.978 --> 00:27:30.081
I just want you to tip that
hammer past its center point.

00:27:30.081 --> 00:27:30.615
Go ahead.

00:27:34.219 --> 00:27:35.720
That's what Peter
was talking about.

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:37.322
So you can hit it really hard.

00:27:37.322 --> 00:27:39.324
- Now I feel like
we've been swindled.

00:27:39.324 --> 00:27:40.458
- Swindled not.

00:27:40.458 --> 00:27:41.526
We have just experienced--

00:27:41.526 --> 00:27:42.627
That's good writing.

00:27:42.627 --> 00:27:43.461
That's good writing.

00:27:43.461 --> 00:27:45.030
- --the power of
compressive strength.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:46.831
It does, however,
have an Achilles heel.

00:27:46.831 --> 00:27:51.803
Take those nippers right
there and nip the backside

00:27:51.803 --> 00:27:53.805
of the tail of this
Prince Rupert drop

00:27:53.805 --> 00:27:54.773
and watch what happens.

00:27:54.773 --> 00:27:56.474
Wait, wait, wait, cue
the high-speed camera.

00:27:56.474 --> 00:27:57.175
OK, here we go.

00:28:01.579 --> 00:28:02.080
- Oh!

00:28:05.216 --> 00:28:05.784
Here it comes.

00:28:11.022 --> 00:28:13.558
- That was even cooler
than I thought it would be.

00:28:13.558 --> 00:28:14.526
[END PLAYBACK]

00:28:14.526 --> 00:28:16.795
So that's what
you just saw live.

00:28:16.795 --> 00:28:20.198
And what that is is
it's a relief of--

00:28:20.198 --> 00:28:21.499
thank you, or not.

00:28:21.499 --> 00:28:22.467
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:23.068
Yeah, sure.

00:28:26.571 --> 00:28:28.640
As Peter said, tempered
glass like in your car,

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:30.642
it's done with air cooling.

00:28:30.642 --> 00:28:33.244
That's literally what
those circles are.

00:28:33.244 --> 00:28:34.846
If you cool it even faster--

00:28:37.482 --> 00:28:39.250
bam, faster cooling.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:42.253
You now know, what does
faster cooling mean?

00:28:42.253 --> 00:28:44.355
Well, if it's going
to form a glass

00:28:44.355 --> 00:28:47.459
you might be all the way
near the melting point,

00:28:47.459 --> 00:28:50.929
and you're going to have an
even-higher volume per mole

00:28:50.929 --> 00:28:53.398
which is going to cause even
more stress within the glass

00:28:53.398 --> 00:28:55.767
which is going to
make the surface very

00:28:55.767 --> 00:28:59.003
strong because it's
all compressed,

00:28:59.003 --> 00:29:01.206
but it's also going to
have a lot of energy in it

00:29:01.206 --> 00:29:02.273
that it wants to release.

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:06.511
Let's see.

00:29:06.511 --> 00:29:08.880
We were here on
Monday, and because I

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:11.616
was talking about other ways
to change the properties--

00:29:11.616 --> 00:29:14.185
in fact, remember, I showed you
a couple of different examples

00:29:14.185 --> 00:29:17.722
of glass, right, a glass
cup and a glass bottle,

00:29:17.722 --> 00:29:21.693
and it turns out there
is not just silica in it.

00:29:21.693 --> 00:29:23.328
There's all these
other things in it,

00:29:23.328 --> 00:29:25.730
and there's many,
many kinds of glass.

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:26.998
And I listed a few here.

00:29:26.998 --> 00:29:28.900
This was from Monday,
so I'm showing it again.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:31.236
And going all the way back
to glass made in ancient Rome

00:29:31.236 --> 00:29:35.373
you'll find that it's got
these other ingredients.

00:29:35.373 --> 00:29:36.174
Why?

00:29:36.174 --> 00:29:39.444
So that's what I want
to talk about next.

00:29:39.444 --> 00:29:40.979
Well, let's use this.

00:29:40.979 --> 00:29:45.850
So if you notice--

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:47.452
and this is where
we left off Monday--

00:29:47.452 --> 00:29:50.822
if you notice something
like calcium oxide, well,

00:29:50.822 --> 00:29:55.794
it's going to go
to Ca2+ and O2-.

00:29:55.794 --> 00:29:58.096
Let's look at Na2O.

00:29:58.096 --> 00:30:04.469
Na2O is going to
go 2Na+ plus O2-.

00:30:04.469 --> 00:30:05.470
How about alumina?

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:08.873
That's on there, Al2O3.

00:30:08.873 --> 00:30:18.049
Well, that's going to
go to 2Al3+ plus 3O2-.

00:30:18.049 --> 00:30:20.952
Notice a pattern.

00:30:20.952 --> 00:30:22.754
The pattern is you get O2-.

00:30:28.026 --> 00:30:30.995
We can understand
why O2- minus has

00:30:30.995 --> 00:30:34.432
been used, why
chemistry has been

00:30:34.432 --> 00:30:40.138
used to change the properties
of glass for millennia.

00:30:40.138 --> 00:30:43.474
And the reason goes
back to the pictures

00:30:43.474 --> 00:30:45.844
that we drew on Monday
where we started

00:30:45.844 --> 00:30:49.681
by talking about the chemistry
of the silicate group.

00:30:49.681 --> 00:30:52.150
So remember Si?

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:53.952
Remember this?

00:30:53.952 --> 00:31:00.058
An SiO4 is happy if it's
got four minus charges.

00:31:00.058 --> 00:31:02.527
Remember that?

00:31:02.527 --> 00:31:05.296
We started with Lewis and
it satisfies the octet.

00:31:05.296 --> 00:31:08.700
But then in glass what
happens is you bridge.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:10.869
And so you have this.

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:13.271
And I won't draw all of them.

00:31:13.271 --> 00:31:15.707
I'll just draw it the
way we drew it before.

00:31:18.509 --> 00:31:21.713
And you have these bridges,
and all of these oxygens

00:31:21.713 --> 00:31:27.318
are bridges in glass.

00:31:27.318 --> 00:31:29.721
So now I'll say, well,
what if I wanted--

00:31:29.721 --> 00:31:36.027
and remember, these are forming
these long, viscous chains.

00:31:36.027 --> 00:31:40.265
What if I wanted to make them
flow a little more easily?

00:31:40.265 --> 00:31:43.167
What if I wanted them
to form shorter chains?

00:31:43.167 --> 00:31:47.472
What if I wanted
to cut the pasta?

00:31:47.472 --> 00:31:49.240
Don't do it in Italy.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:51.509
They'll get really upset.

00:31:51.509 --> 00:31:53.411
But Americans are OK with it.

00:31:53.411 --> 00:31:56.547
If you need to cut the pasta,
it's OK in this country.

00:31:56.547 --> 00:32:00.318
But if I need to cut the pasta
in chemistry and it's glass

00:32:00.318 --> 00:32:04.589
that I'm cutting,
O2- is my knife.

00:32:04.589 --> 00:32:07.625
It's my chemical scissors.

00:32:07.625 --> 00:32:11.930
Chemical scissors, I like
that-- chemical scissors.

00:32:15.099 --> 00:32:15.566
Why?

00:32:15.566 --> 00:32:18.036
Well, you can see
because I go back.

00:32:18.036 --> 00:32:19.437
What did the oxygen do?

00:32:19.437 --> 00:32:23.675
By bridging it didn't
need that extra electron.

00:32:23.675 --> 00:32:27.879
But if I bring now
something like Na2O--

00:32:27.879 --> 00:32:29.380
so I've got an O2-.

00:32:29.380 --> 00:32:32.350
Now I'm going to add an O2-.

00:32:35.086 --> 00:32:38.957
Then what will happen is
you're going to cut this.

00:32:38.957 --> 00:32:42.293
So now I'll just draw the--

00:32:42.293 --> 00:32:43.695
I'll see if I can--

00:32:43.695 --> 00:32:48.399
OK, so now I've added not just
an oxygen atom but the two

00:32:48.399 --> 00:32:53.037
charges that each of them needed
before they bridged, you see,

00:32:53.037 --> 00:32:54.973
to be happy?

00:32:54.973 --> 00:32:57.675
And so now this can have
one of those charges,

00:32:57.675 --> 00:33:03.181
and over here you
can have another one,

00:33:03.181 --> 00:33:07.018
and so on and so on.

00:33:07.018 --> 00:33:09.887
But see, now I've cut.

00:33:09.887 --> 00:33:14.292
I've cut the glass pasta,
and I've done it chemically

00:33:14.292 --> 00:33:16.027
and I've done it
from understanding

00:33:16.027 --> 00:33:18.296
of octet and Lewis,
from basic chemistry

00:33:18.296 --> 00:33:20.164
principles that we've learned.

00:33:20.164 --> 00:33:21.065
Where's the sodium go?

00:33:21.065 --> 00:33:22.967
Well, they'll be like a sodium.

00:33:22.967 --> 00:33:25.970
They'll want to hang
out close by these.

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:27.572
There will be these
ions in there.

00:33:27.572 --> 00:33:29.907
These sodium ions will
be in there still, right?

00:33:29.907 --> 00:33:34.078
If it was Na2O, if
it's something else

00:33:34.078 --> 00:33:36.547
and it's a different kind of
ion that might be leftover,

00:33:36.547 --> 00:33:39.951
but the main thing is I've
delivered the chemistry

00:33:39.951 --> 00:33:41.152
needed to cut this.

00:33:41.152 --> 00:33:43.554
And this is called
chain scission.

00:33:43.554 --> 00:33:50.828
There's a term for it, chain
scission, the technical term

00:33:50.828 --> 00:33:54.232
for cutting glass pasta.

00:33:54.232 --> 00:33:55.700
Well, this is powerful.

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:59.404
This is very powerful
because if I can cut this--

00:33:59.404 --> 00:34:01.873
those are the silicate
groups, right?

00:34:01.873 --> 00:34:06.310
And if I can cut them, then I
can change another property.

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:09.813
Look, I can change
the viscosity.

00:34:09.813 --> 00:34:15.719
I've hit another one of those
three reasons why glass forms.

00:34:15.719 --> 00:34:18.089
And so I'll show you a
slide in a minute that

00:34:18.089 --> 00:34:19.690
shows you the change
in the viscosity,

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:24.195
but just to give you a
sense of what can happen,

00:34:24.195 --> 00:34:26.397
how does that impact?

00:34:26.397 --> 00:34:27.264
How does that impact?

00:34:27.264 --> 00:34:35.572
Well, for silica crystal,
the melting point

00:34:35.572 --> 00:34:54.458
is greater than 1,200 C. For Tg
of soda-lime glass, it's 500 C.

00:34:54.458 --> 00:34:59.063
So you can imagine now
I've got another mechanism.

00:34:59.063 --> 00:35:01.899
I've got another way,
not just processing,

00:35:01.899 --> 00:35:06.137
not just cooling
rate, but chemistry

00:35:06.137 --> 00:35:08.406
to mess with this curve.

00:35:08.406 --> 00:35:10.208
Because you can
imagine now that I

00:35:10.208 --> 00:35:15.046
might be able to get to lower
and lower transition points.

00:35:15.046 --> 00:35:15.713
You see this?

00:35:15.713 --> 00:35:18.382
I might be able to get to lower
and lower transition points

00:35:18.382 --> 00:35:23.988
if these things can move faster
and not get locked into a solid

00:35:23.988 --> 00:35:30.228
as easily if I add more and
more of these chemical scissors.

00:35:30.228 --> 00:35:34.465
So maybe this one, now instead
of being cooled slower, maybe

00:35:34.465 --> 00:35:40.972
this one had more
soda than this one.

00:35:40.972 --> 00:35:42.907
Maybe now they're
cooled at the same rate

00:35:42.907 --> 00:35:46.777
but I've changed the
amount of impurities,

00:35:46.777 --> 00:35:51.182
the amount of oxygen, O2- very
specifically, that I've put in.

00:35:51.182 --> 00:35:57.555
So it's another way now
to change this curve.

00:35:57.555 --> 00:36:01.058
Now, these are called--

00:36:01.058 --> 00:36:03.261
the thing that forms
the glass-- and we're

00:36:03.261 --> 00:36:05.796
really only talking about
silica as our glass.

00:36:05.796 --> 00:36:09.567
You can make other types of
glass not made from silica.

00:36:09.567 --> 00:36:11.269
We're talking about
silica in this class.

00:36:11.269 --> 00:36:15.973
So silicon is the atom that
is making those long spaghetti

00:36:15.973 --> 00:36:19.010
strands, and it's called
the network former.

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.078
Silicon is called
the network former.

00:36:21.078 --> 00:36:24.582
And then the thing
that I put in there--

00:36:24.582 --> 00:36:27.451
the thing that I put in there
that modified the network

00:36:27.451 --> 00:36:29.420
is called the network modifier.

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:34.125
So you can see on this
graph that what you've got

00:36:34.125 --> 00:36:38.296
is your bridging,
the bridging there

00:36:38.296 --> 00:36:41.499
that's going between
two network formers.

00:36:41.499 --> 00:36:45.036
And then you've got
these atoms that

00:36:45.036 --> 00:36:48.639
are hanging out near a place
where you've broken a bridge.

00:36:48.639 --> 00:36:50.208
So those would be modifiers.

00:36:50.208 --> 00:36:51.676
Those are called
network modifiers.

00:36:51.676 --> 00:36:54.745
These are just terms that
you will hear and see

00:36:54.745 --> 00:36:57.782
when you think about glass--

00:36:57.782 --> 00:37:00.451
network formers,
network modifiers.

00:37:00.451 --> 00:37:02.987
The chemistry that you introduce
is modifying the network,

00:37:02.987 --> 00:37:06.691
and it's what is done in
many, many different ways

00:37:06.691 --> 00:37:08.960
with many, many
different [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:08.960 --> 00:37:11.529
to change the
properties of glass.

00:37:11.529 --> 00:37:13.231
And so here's the
curve that shows

00:37:13.231 --> 00:37:17.168
you another kind of important
way of looking at this.

00:37:17.168 --> 00:37:19.470
And I don't need you to
remember all of these terms,

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:20.938
but I want to show
you because this

00:37:20.938 --> 00:37:22.907
is how glass is engineered.

00:37:22.907 --> 00:37:27.144
Remember, today the topic is
how do we change the properties?

00:37:27.144 --> 00:37:28.512
We talked about
the cooling rate.

00:37:28.512 --> 00:37:31.682
Now we're talking
about adding chemicals.

00:37:31.682 --> 00:37:34.518
And so now you're going
to work with the glass

00:37:34.518 --> 00:37:37.054
to do something with it.

00:37:37.054 --> 00:37:39.457
If you're going to do that,
this is one of the first plots

00:37:39.457 --> 00:37:41.726
you'll look up.

00:37:41.726 --> 00:37:43.728
And it shows you-- it
plots the temperature.

00:37:43.728 --> 00:37:46.497
This really doesn't
work at all--

00:37:46.497 --> 00:37:49.233
the temperature on one axis--
so that's like the x-axis--

00:37:49.233 --> 00:37:51.202
and on the y-axis is the
viscosity because now

00:37:51.202 --> 00:37:53.804
that's the property that we're
changing, right, the viscosity?

00:37:56.641 --> 00:37:58.676
So there's silica up
on the upper right.

00:37:58.676 --> 00:37:59.710
Can you see that?

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:03.948
So fused silica, that's
basically almost quartz.

00:38:03.948 --> 00:38:05.783
That's basically quartz.

00:38:05.783 --> 00:38:10.321
So you are really close
to making a nice crystal,

00:38:10.321 --> 00:38:15.293
but notice how high of viscosity
you have versus temperature.

00:38:15.293 --> 00:38:18.329
Notice that you're at this very,
very high value for viscosity.

00:38:18.329 --> 00:38:20.865
It's a really thick material.

00:38:20.865 --> 00:38:22.833
And people care a
lot about viscosity

00:38:22.833 --> 00:38:23.901
when they work with glass.

00:38:23.901 --> 00:38:24.402
Why?

00:38:24.402 --> 00:38:28.472
Because you need to
know, for example,

00:38:28.472 --> 00:38:30.708
is the viscosity at a point
where I can work with it?

00:38:30.708 --> 00:38:31.909
Can I shape it?

00:38:31.909 --> 00:38:33.110
That's the bottom.

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:35.146
That's the region that
says working range.

00:38:35.146 --> 00:38:36.314
I can still shape it.

00:38:36.314 --> 00:38:37.181
I can make a window.

00:38:39.917 --> 00:38:42.119
There it flows under
its own weight.

00:38:42.119 --> 00:38:45.456
So now I can shape it and
it will lose the shape.

00:38:45.456 --> 00:38:48.659
That's kind of important to know
if I'm making a glass animal.

00:38:51.329 --> 00:38:54.665
There its stress can
still be relieved.

00:38:54.665 --> 00:38:56.267
Remaining stress can
still be relieved,

00:38:56.267 --> 00:38:58.102
and up there you
can rapidly cool it

00:38:58.102 --> 00:38:59.970
without introducing new defects.

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:02.807
But look at the tradeoff
between the temperature

00:39:02.807 --> 00:39:06.143
that you have to go to and the
viscosity and the viscosity

00:39:06.143 --> 00:39:07.111
that you have.

00:39:07.111 --> 00:39:10.381
So if you go now from
this almost quartz

00:39:10.381 --> 00:39:11.615
all the way down--

00:39:11.615 --> 00:39:14.618
OK, there you take a little
bit of stuff and you put it in,

00:39:14.618 --> 00:39:16.454
but it's still 96% silica.

00:39:16.454 --> 00:39:18.189
And now you go to
boiled silicate.

00:39:18.189 --> 00:39:23.594
Remember, it's like 75%, 80%
silica plus other additives.

00:39:23.594 --> 00:39:26.831
And look at how much you've
changed the temperature.

00:39:26.831 --> 00:39:28.733
So I can now work
with this material

00:39:28.733 --> 00:39:34.271
below 1,000 C. That has huge
consequences for industry,

00:39:34.271 --> 00:39:37.308
for actually making
different things out

00:39:37.308 --> 00:39:40.444
of glass, which we
now do regularly.

00:39:40.444 --> 00:39:42.713
And the Romans did it too,
but they didn't know why.

00:39:42.713 --> 00:39:44.949
They said, well, if you add
a little bit of this dirt,

00:39:44.949 --> 00:39:46.117
something different happens.

00:39:46.117 --> 00:39:48.119
Let's try a little
bit of that one.

00:39:48.119 --> 00:39:49.053
Now we know.

00:39:49.053 --> 00:39:50.554
We know why.

00:39:53.257 --> 00:39:55.059
And so this curve
now is something

00:39:55.059 --> 00:39:58.562
that we understand and we
understand how to control it.

00:39:58.562 --> 00:40:06.270
And if you go back to the
example of making glass tough,

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:10.641
I was just showing you these
ions, these sodium ions.

00:40:10.641 --> 00:40:12.176
They're left in there.

00:40:12.176 --> 00:40:16.914
Well, it turns out that you
can put ions into glass,

00:40:16.914 --> 00:40:19.917
and maybe you're doing it
to change the viscosity

00:40:19.917 --> 00:40:22.620
so the thing stays
liquid for longer,

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:26.357
and you can maybe work with
it in different regimes.

00:40:26.357 --> 00:40:28.125
But maybe you're putting
the ions in there

00:40:28.125 --> 00:40:31.395
to do something else.

00:40:31.395 --> 00:40:34.965
And so actually there's
a whole another thing

00:40:34.965 --> 00:40:39.637
you can do once you have ions
in the glass like sodium.

00:40:39.637 --> 00:40:42.606
You can substitute them out.

00:40:42.606 --> 00:40:45.242
Imagine that I
have-- that's what

00:40:45.242 --> 00:40:46.610
this next picture is showing.

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:50.281
Imagine that I've
got a glass in here,

00:40:50.281 --> 00:40:53.617
and those red
circles are sodium.

00:40:53.617 --> 00:40:56.887
But now I've introduced a
different ion on the surface.

00:40:56.887 --> 00:40:58.889
That's potassium.

00:40:58.889 --> 00:40:59.623
What happens?

00:40:59.623 --> 00:41:02.893
Well, if I have a high
concentration of potassium

00:41:02.893 --> 00:41:04.728
and I put it on a
surface of glass that

00:41:04.728 --> 00:41:07.364
has a bunch of
sodium in it, so now

00:41:07.364 --> 00:41:12.036
I've got a glass with
sodium ions in it.

00:41:12.036 --> 00:41:17.942
And I expose it to
a potassium bath.

00:41:17.942 --> 00:41:22.112
So now I've got
potassium up here.

00:41:22.112 --> 00:41:25.549
And what happens is if the
concentration is high enough

00:41:25.549 --> 00:41:27.818
and the conditions are
right, I can get potassium

00:41:27.818 --> 00:41:31.322
to go in for the sodium.

00:41:31.322 --> 00:41:34.391
And so actually now
I can substitute.

00:41:34.391 --> 00:41:36.560
So these were here.

00:41:36.560 --> 00:41:40.064
I can now substitute
those for potassium.

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:46.537
Well, but this is a big
deal because potassium

00:41:46.537 --> 00:41:48.806
is bigger than sodium.

00:41:48.806 --> 00:41:53.077
And so if I am able to on
the surface, let's say,

00:41:53.077 --> 00:41:56.113
put something bigger
in there, then you

00:41:56.113 --> 00:41:59.316
can imagine that it's
pushing the other stuff

00:41:59.316 --> 00:42:01.051
into each other.

00:42:01.051 --> 00:42:04.221
That's causing the same stress.

00:42:04.221 --> 00:42:15.266
K+ plus bigger than sodium, and
this causes this huge surface

00:42:15.266 --> 00:42:15.766
tension.

00:42:18.769 --> 00:42:24.909
Same principle as tempering,
but now I've done it chemically.

00:42:24.909 --> 00:42:27.144
This is Gorilla Glass.

00:42:27.144 --> 00:42:29.780
This is all phone screens.

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:34.018
This is what they do because
you can get really, really

00:42:34.018 --> 00:42:35.819
hard glass this way.

00:42:35.819 --> 00:42:38.255
And if you go back to these
guys, they have a little--

00:42:38.255 --> 00:42:39.757
and this is just a
very short video.

00:42:39.757 --> 00:42:43.093
They've got this sort of longer
videos on the Corning website

00:42:43.093 --> 00:42:44.929
that talk about this,
and they shoot bullets

00:42:44.929 --> 00:42:46.297
through Gorilla Glass.

00:42:46.297 --> 00:42:48.799
You can make glass
really strong with just

00:42:48.799 --> 00:42:51.769
chemical substitutions, and
all you're thinking about

00:42:51.769 --> 00:42:53.103
is atom size.

00:42:53.103 --> 00:42:55.739
Because by putting
this larger atom in,

00:42:55.739 --> 00:42:58.876
you cause this same kind
of compressive stress

00:42:58.876 --> 00:42:59.743
at the surface.

00:42:59.743 --> 00:43:00.411
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:43:00.411 --> 00:43:03.180
- Gorilla Glass, it's
been refined over time,

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:06.083
but like all Gorilla Glass
variants that came before it,

00:43:06.083 --> 00:43:08.352
it is compressive-strength
glass.

00:43:08.352 --> 00:43:10.921
But it's not made in the same
way as we just demonstrated,

00:43:10.921 --> 00:43:12.022
the rapid-cooling method.

00:43:12.022 --> 00:43:15.726
No, instead Corning uses
an ion-exchange process.

00:43:15.726 --> 00:43:18.028
To break it down
simply, the surface ion

00:43:18.028 --> 00:43:20.664
particles that naturally
form during the manufacture

00:43:20.664 --> 00:43:23.334
are replaced with
larger ion particles.

00:43:23.334 --> 00:43:25.636
Once exchanged, the
larger ion particles

00:43:25.636 --> 00:43:27.838
create the same sort
of inward pressure

00:43:27.838 --> 00:43:29.873
that we see on the
Prince Rupert drop.

00:43:29.873 --> 00:43:33.510
And with this method they are
able to control and manage

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:34.378
the resulting--

00:43:34.378 --> 00:43:35.646
[END PLAYBACK]

00:43:35.646 --> 00:43:38.082
The resulting what?

00:43:38.082 --> 00:43:41.085
What was he going to say?

00:43:41.085 --> 00:43:42.686
I don't know actually.

00:43:42.686 --> 00:43:43.420
I don't remember.

00:43:43.420 --> 00:43:45.689
I have to go watch it now.

00:43:45.689 --> 00:43:47.224
So that's just a
video showing you

00:43:47.224 --> 00:43:49.226
what I just showed you,
which is that that's yet

00:43:49.226 --> 00:43:52.730
another way of engineering
the properties of glass,

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:54.565
and there are so many more.

00:43:56.667 --> 00:43:58.268
One of the points I
want to make today

00:43:58.268 --> 00:43:59.903
about how to engineer
glass-- so we're talking

00:43:59.903 --> 00:44:01.405
about how to change this curve.

00:44:01.405 --> 00:44:04.441
Maybe we change the
viscosity by adding O2

00:44:04.441 --> 00:44:07.611
which changes the curve here.

00:44:07.611 --> 00:44:08.979
Maybe we change
the cooling rate.

00:44:12.816 --> 00:44:16.620
Maybe I can use additives,
ions in different ways.

00:44:16.620 --> 00:44:18.122
But it turns out
that we have come

00:44:18.122 --> 00:44:20.891
to a place where we can
engineer every single one

00:44:20.891 --> 00:44:22.359
of these properties.

00:44:22.359 --> 00:44:25.229
We can make conducting glass,
even though it's usually

00:44:25.229 --> 00:44:28.399
been and usually is insulating.

00:44:28.399 --> 00:44:31.101
We can make chemically
active glass.

00:44:31.101 --> 00:44:34.805
As you just saw, we can change
the mechanical properties.

00:44:34.805 --> 00:44:37.408
There are now flexible
rolls of glass

00:44:37.408 --> 00:44:40.144
that look literally like Saran
wrap almost, but they're glass.

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:45.282
And so it all comes
down to the pasta.

00:44:45.282 --> 00:44:48.352
And so where we are, if
we use the pasta analogy--

00:44:48.352 --> 00:44:51.822
which as you, I think, can
understand I really like

00:44:51.822 --> 00:44:54.992
because I really like pasta.

00:44:54.992 --> 00:44:56.860
This is a great
analogy for glass

00:44:56.860 --> 00:45:00.864
because it's like
in the Roman time,

00:45:00.864 --> 00:45:04.201
all we had was one
sauce, everything.

00:45:04.201 --> 00:45:06.003
Just mix it all in--

00:45:06.003 --> 00:45:11.642
meatballs, pesto,
red sauce, carbonara.

00:45:11.642 --> 00:45:14.044
I can go on.

00:45:14.044 --> 00:45:15.045
And that's all they did.

00:45:15.045 --> 00:45:17.381
They just didn't know why
things were happening.

00:45:17.381 --> 00:45:21.118
But now that we know about the
electronic structure of atoms,

00:45:21.118 --> 00:45:27.124
we can make glass into any
delicious pasta dish we want,

00:45:27.124 --> 00:45:28.559
right?

00:45:28.559 --> 00:45:31.495
OK, don't eat it, but
you know what I mean.

00:45:31.495 --> 00:45:33.697
And so I'll give you
a couple of examples.

00:45:33.697 --> 00:45:35.532
So the point is the
last 20 centuries,

00:45:35.532 --> 00:45:37.267
we were really just
getting warmed up

00:45:37.267 --> 00:45:38.569
when it comes to this material.

00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:46.677
What the recent understanding
of how to engineer this material

00:45:46.677 --> 00:45:49.947
has led to are real
breakthrough ideas.

00:45:49.947 --> 00:45:54.284
And so I'll show you just a
couple in my why this matters.

00:45:54.284 --> 00:45:56.954
Because glass is, after
all, made of-- there

00:45:56.954 --> 00:45:58.622
it is up there in
case you can't see it.

00:45:58.622 --> 00:46:03.227
This is the abundance of
elements versus atomic number,

00:46:03.227 --> 00:46:05.529
and you notice the
very top two elements

00:46:05.529 --> 00:46:11.268
are silicon and oxygen. And so
it would be really nice if we

00:46:11.268 --> 00:46:13.403
could make a lot of
stuff out of these really

00:46:13.403 --> 00:46:14.772
abundant cheap elements.

00:46:14.772 --> 00:46:17.174
Think sand.

00:46:17.174 --> 00:46:21.178
Could I take sand and make
a lot of stuff out of it?

00:46:21.178 --> 00:46:22.980
Well, not if I can't control.

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:27.785
Not if I have to heat it up to
3,000 degrees or its viscosity

00:46:27.785 --> 00:46:30.354
isn't what I want or it doesn't
give me the properties I want,

00:46:30.354 --> 00:46:32.322
but that has changed.

00:46:32.322 --> 00:46:34.858
That has fundamentally changed.

00:46:34.858 --> 00:46:36.994
So we can now look at
materials like this,

00:46:36.994 --> 00:46:39.229
these super-abundant materials,
and we can completely

00:46:39.229 --> 00:46:39.830
rethink them.

00:46:39.830 --> 00:46:41.431
And I'll give you one example.

00:46:41.431 --> 00:46:42.900
It's already a few
years old, but I

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:46.069
think it's just a
really cool idea, which

00:46:46.069 --> 00:46:47.871
is called the Solar Sinter.

00:46:47.871 --> 00:46:50.808
And here he's
developed this machine

00:46:50.808 --> 00:46:52.843
that is entirely solar powered.

00:46:52.843 --> 00:46:54.912
They're solar cells
for the electricity.

00:46:54.912 --> 00:46:59.616
And it's a focused beam
of light from the sun that

00:46:59.616 --> 00:47:02.519
gets hot enough to
engineer the glass.

00:47:02.519 --> 00:47:05.522
So he takes sand from
the desert that he's in.

00:47:05.522 --> 00:47:08.425
He puts it in a container,
and he's got a 3D printer

00:47:08.425 --> 00:47:09.593
that he's made.

00:47:09.593 --> 00:47:11.495
It's entirely solar powered.

00:47:11.495 --> 00:47:15.699
There's no fossil fuels, but
he can take sand and turn it

00:47:15.699 --> 00:47:18.569
into something structural.

00:47:18.569 --> 00:47:21.171
And so there's a
vase that he's made,

00:47:21.171 --> 00:47:22.673
and you can take that out.

00:47:22.673 --> 00:47:24.374
And again, that's
just the beginning

00:47:24.374 --> 00:47:27.010
of rethinking what we could
do with these super-abundant

00:47:27.010 --> 00:47:27.845
materials.

00:47:27.845 --> 00:47:32.449
Here's an example from also a
few years ago from an MIT lab.

00:47:32.449 --> 00:47:35.686
This is Neri Oxman's lab, and
she's in the Mediated Matter

00:47:35.686 --> 00:47:40.224
lab here at MIT, and she's
developed a 3D printer.

00:47:40.224 --> 00:47:40.891
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:47:40.891 --> 00:47:42.693
[MUSIC PLAYBACK]

00:47:42.693 --> 00:47:47.698
And so there it is printing
something with glass.

00:47:47.698 --> 00:47:51.368
And she can print lots of
different designs now using

00:47:51.368 --> 00:47:52.069
glass.

00:47:52.069 --> 00:47:54.404
She has a cool video.

00:47:54.404 --> 00:47:56.139
And there is the printhead.

00:48:03.580 --> 00:48:38.682
And again, 1,900 Fahrenheit.

00:48:38.682 --> 00:48:41.618
I'm not sure that I
want a 1,900 Fahrenheit

00:48:41.618 --> 00:48:44.721
printer on my desktop.

00:48:44.721 --> 00:48:48.258
But as you can imagine,
what they had to do--

00:48:48.258 --> 00:48:50.594
I'll just give you one more
example and then we'll stop.

00:48:50.594 --> 00:48:52.429
What they had to do is
understand everything

00:48:52.429 --> 00:48:53.430
we've just talked about.

00:48:53.430 --> 00:48:55.999
How do you engineer the
viscosity, the melting point

00:48:55.999 --> 00:48:58.235
to make it all work
in a 3D printer?

00:48:58.235 --> 00:48:59.870
The last point I'll
give you is this.

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:00.537
I love this.

00:49:00.537 --> 00:49:01.872
This is from a few years ago.

00:49:01.872 --> 00:49:04.775
It's a group in Japan
that made a glass that

00:49:04.775 --> 00:49:08.111
is as strong as steel.

00:49:08.111 --> 00:49:10.080
And what they talk about--
this is what I like.

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:12.649
They say just think of a world
where your smartphone wouldn't

00:49:12.649 --> 00:49:13.150
shatter.

00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:14.985
OK, cool.

00:49:14.985 --> 00:49:17.921
Buildings could be bolstered
against natural disasters, even

00:49:17.921 --> 00:49:18.622
cooler.

00:49:18.622 --> 00:49:20.257
And then somehow
they bring it down.

00:49:20.257 --> 00:49:22.292
Wine glasses are
reassuringly safe.

00:49:22.292 --> 00:49:24.061
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:24.061 --> 00:49:25.829
Was that really a problem?

00:49:25.829 --> 00:49:27.564
I don't know.

00:49:27.564 --> 00:49:30.367
And what they did,
fabrication was

00:49:30.367 --> 00:49:33.337
conducted using an aerodynamic
levitation furnace where

00:49:33.337 --> 00:49:36.106
ingredients were floated
in the air using oxygen gas

00:49:36.106 --> 00:49:38.875
and melted together
using CO2 lasers,

00:49:38.875 --> 00:49:41.812
and they get a transparent
superglass with 50% alumina.

00:49:41.812 --> 00:49:45.816
That was so hard to do
because the aluminum didn't

00:49:45.816 --> 00:49:47.084
want to be a glass.

00:49:47.084 --> 00:49:51.421
It wanted to go and
become a metal, a crystal.

00:49:51.421 --> 00:49:52.889
But by doing it
this way, they were

00:49:52.889 --> 00:49:56.193
able to capture it
in the disorder.

00:49:56.193 --> 00:49:57.427
All right, have a great night.

00:49:57.427 --> 00:49:59.329
See you guys on Friday.

